    "body": "Tiles stored their label and mold even though both are determined by form. Each serialized tile carried redundant strings and precedence numbers so operator-precedence changes required migrating every serialized file, and nothing prevented label/mold mismatches.\r\n\r\nNow a tile is `{id, form, sort, shards, children}` where `form = Compound(family) | Tok(token) | TokInfix(token)`, with 92 form families (label x shape-role) plus free-text tokens, and label/mold are derived. `TokInfix` is a technical detail that will die with virtual grout.\r\n\r\nThis isn't quite a pure refactor, but my hope is that there are no observable logic changes.\r\n\r\n- Serialized syntax shrinks ~40%, and precedence/mold changes no longer require migrations.\r\n- MakeTerm dispatches on constructors instead of token-string lists. This technically causes some behavior changes in edge cases, but I have never seen the relevant behaviors in the wild.\r\n- Exercise/derivation examples now ship as serialized-zipper text literals rather than OCaml literals, so they survive representation changes; slide files were regenerated id- and refractor-preservingly.\r\n- The stored `sort` is an explicitly isolated local cache (well-sorted <=> `mold_of(form, sort).out == sort`); experimental follow up work will hopefully make sort a derived property, eliminating this field\r\n- The grammar's string layer now lives in the language library (src/language/grammar/), including Token, Label, and FormId (the family enums and the per-family label table), following the existing Sort re-export pattern. This deduplicates the token/string mirrors that src/language previously hand-maintained\r\n- TyDi's suggestion-type tables are now derived from the grammar at startup instead of hand-maintained.\r\n",
    "created_at": "2026-07-22T08:18:19Z",
